    {
      "id": 162450,
      "name": "Ironic",
      "username": "Ironic",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ironic/{size}/2171_2.png",
      "created_at": "2016-02-17T19:17:16.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>I'd say the Sentinels are the best call mechanics-wise, since they're so One-Shot dependent.&nbsp; Not sure how they line up flavor-wise, since they're mostly a pretty obscure group in-setting.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>But now you've gotten me wondering about characters like The Scholar and Chrono-Ranger, who probably <em>could</em> work in this format, but operating very differently (Ranger cares more about damaging One-Shots and less anout Equipment, Scholar is pushed towards Iron Form over Liquid Energy).&nbsp; I might just have to try that!</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>ExPat is bad, but I'd say Unity is the worst option in this format.&nbsp; With no Guns and no Golems, you've got one character who can only play RPG Launcher and Flak Jacket, and another who can only play Brainstorm.&nbsp; Hoping to draw a second Brainstorm.&nbsp; Oh, and I guess Powered Shockwave, should there be a damage boost out.&nbsp; Sure, the Adept only has Vernal Sonata, but a few Vernal Sonatas can win games.&nbsp; And I guess there's always Silver Shadow to activate the odd Accompany.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>So intriguing!&nbsp; I think I'll play my next game this way, using Tachyon, Sentinels, Scholar, and Legacy.&nbsp; I'll probably take the coward's path and start with someone easy like Ambuscade, then if that goes well, move on to the serious villains.</p>",
      "post_number": 5,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:43:16.513Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
